{"id": "live-295", "title": "GSD Contracting, LLC v. All American Builders & Debris Removal, Inc.: Florida Appeals Court Affirms $71,582.98 Retainage After Mold Dispute on Hotel Project", "category": "construction-defect", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-10-14", "jurisdiction": "District Court of Appeal of Florida, Fourth District", "state": "FL", "amount": "$71,582.98 retainage affirmed; $45,300 unperformed-work award reversed", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://storage.courtlistener.com/pdf/2026/10/14/gsd_contracting_llc_v._all_american_builders__debris_removal_inc..pdf", "summary": "On October 14, 2026, Florida's Fourth District Court of Appeal affirmed a trial court judgment that GSD Contracting, LLC breached its subcontract with All American Builders & Debris Removal, Inc. by withholding a $71,582.98 retainage owed on a five-story hotel project, after a mold-remediation dispute led GSD to terminate the subcontractor. The court reversed a separate $45,300 the trial court had also awarded for work All American never performed, since All American had never sought that amount and the subcontract itself barred recovery for unperformed work after a termination for convenience.", "significance": "The decision is a reminder that termination-for-convenience and retainage clauses are enforced literally: absent an express termination-for-cause provision, a general contractor cannot rely on an alleged subcontractor default, including a dispute over remediation work, as grounds to withhold a retainage that has otherwise become payable under the contract's own terms. General contractors stepping into distressed projects as a replacement GC, as GSD did here, should confirm their subcontracts actually contain the for-cause termination and offset rights they intend to invoke before withholding payment.", "judge": "Spencer D. Levine", "tags": ["construction", "subcontractor", "retainage", "mold", "hotel"]}
